  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 143 AM EST Wed Nov 4 2026\n\nAn approaching low pressure system over the MS Valley region will \nbring gusty winds to the mountains and foothills and widespread \nrainfall to our area today and tonight. So far, winds in the usual \nwind-prone places in the mountains and foothills have been in the 35-\n45 mph range. A few spots like Cove Mountain and Camp Creek could \nreach 50-60 mph, but most areas in the Wind Advisory will stay under \n50 mph. \n\nLight precip has been spreading into the area already, but the \nmoderate rain associated with the cold front will arrive later in \nthe morning, around 12Z or so. There is some weak instability that \ncould allow for a few isolated thunderstorms, but overall, the \ndynamics with this system are not particularly impressive. The front \nwill be slow to progress through the area as the closed upper low \nmoves over the Great Lakes, and this slow movement will allow for \nsome needed rainfall amounts of a half to one inch through Wednesday \nnight. Some spots with orographic enhancement could see higher \namounts. The preceding dry conditions make the chance of flooding \nlow, but it cannot be ruled out completely in some flood-prone or \nurban areas. \n\nTemperatures behind the front on Thursday don't drop much, with \nhighs still in the 70s. A stronger push of cold air comes on Friday \nnight with the passage of a deeper upper trough and a secondary cold \nfront. This will bring highs in the 60s and lows in the 30s for the \nweekend.",
